codeExample.bad:
/* HTML has Question then Answer in that order, but CSS visually reverses them for sighted users. 
   A screen reader still announces "Question" first, then "Answer" — matching sighted intent —
   but here the visual reversal makes sighted users read Answer before Question while DOM order
   says the opposite, so the two experiences diverge. */
.qa-pair {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row-reverse; /* Answer renders visually first (left), Question visually second (right) */
}

codeExample.good:
/* Reorder the HTML itself so DOM order matches the intended reading order,
   then let CSS lay elements out normally — sighted and screen reader users get the same sequence. */
.qa-pair {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;
}
